e:.9em;line-height:1.4;margin:0 auto 4px auto;width:100%}.mw-parser-output .wst-header-left{float:left}</style><div class=\"ws-header wst-header-structure wst-unknown wst-header ws-header ws-noexport noprint dynlayout-exempt\"><div class=\"wst-header-mainblock wst-unknown-mainblock headertemplate\"><div class=\"wst-header-back searchaux wst-unknown-back\"><div class=\"wst-header-back-arrow\">\u2190</div><div class=\"wst-header-back-link\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments/Book_3/Chapter_13\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments/Book 3/Chapter 13\">Book 3, Chapter 13</a></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-central-cell wst-unknown-central-cell\"><span class=\"wst-header-title wst-unknown-title\"><span class=\"wst-header-title-text\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments\">The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments</a></span> (<span class=\"wst-header-year-text\">1877</span>)<br class=\"wst-header-title-break\" /><span class=\"contributor-text\">by&#160;<span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"/wiki/Author:Epictetus\" title=\"Author:Epictetus\">Epictetus</a></span></span>, <span class=\"contributor-text\">translated by&#160;<span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"/wiki/Author:George_Long\" title=\"Author:George Long\">George Long</a></span></span><div class=\"header-section-text\">Book 3, Chapter 14</div></span></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward searchaux wst-unknown-forward\"><div class=\"wst-header-forward-link\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments/Book_3/Chapter_15\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments/Book 3/Chapter 15\">Book 3, Chapter 15</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-author\">Epictetus</span><span id=\"ws-article-id\">4536893</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments\">The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments</a> \u2014 Book 3, Chapter 14</span><span id=\"ws-year\">1877</span><span id=\"ws-translator\">George Long</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 1</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r13049624/mw-parser-output/.prp-pages-output\">.mw-parser-output .prp-pages-output .wst-heading{font-size:120%;text-align:center;margin-top:2em}</style><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"233\" data-page-number=\"233\" data-page-name=\"Page:Discourses of Epictetus.djvu/287\" data-page-index=\"287\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Discourses&#95;of&#95;Epictetus.djvu/287\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;287\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15620827\">.mw-parser-output .wst-center{text-align:center;margin:0 auto}.mw-parser-output .wst-center.wst-center-nomargin>p{margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0}</style><div class=\"wst-center tiInherit\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size:120%;\">CHAPTER XIV.</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r15620827\" /><div class=\"wst-center tiInherit\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">certain miscellaneous matters.</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">As</span> bad<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-1\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> tragic actors cannot sing alone, but in company with many: so some persons cannot walk about alone. Man, if you are anything, both walk alone and talk to yourself, and do not hide yourself in the chorus. Examine a little at last, look around, stir yourself up, that you may know who you are.\n</p><p>When a man drinks water, or does anything for the sake of practice (discipline), whenever there is an opportunity he tells it to all: 'I drink water.' Is it for this that you drink water, for the purpose of drinking water? Man, if it is good for you to drink, drink; but if not, you are acting ridiculously. But if it is good for you and you do drink, say nothing about it to those who are displeased with water-drinkers. What then, do you wish to please these very men?\n</p><p>Of things that are done some are done with a final purpose (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431475\">.mw-parser-output .wst-lang-grc{font-family:GentiumPlus;font-feature-settings:\"cv78\"on,\"cv83\"on}</style><span class=\"wst-lang wst-lang-grc\" lang=\"grc\" dir=\"ltr\">\u03c0\u03c1\u03bf\u03b7\u03b3\u03bf\u03c5\u03bc\u03ad\u03bd\u03c9\u03c2</span>), some according to occasion, others with a certain reference to circumstances, others for the purpose of complying with others, and some according to a fixed scheme of life.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-2\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-2\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup>\n</p><p>You must root out of men these two things, arrogance (pride) and distrust. Arrogance then is the opinion that you want nothing (are deficient in nothing): but distrust is the opinion that you cannot be happy when so many circumstances surround you. Arrogance is removed by confutation; and Socrates was the first who practised this. And (to know) that the thing is not impossible inquire and seek. This search will do you no harm; and in a manner this is philosophizing, to seek how it is possible to employ desire and aversion (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r15431475\" /><span class=\"wst-lang wst-lang-grc\" lang=\"grc\" dir=\"ltr\">\u1f10\u03ba\u03ba\u03bb\u03af\u03c3\u03b5\u03b9</span>) without impediment.\n</p><p>I am superior to you, for my father is a man of consular rank. Another says, I have been a tribune, but you have&#32;<span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"234\" data-page-number=\"234\" data-page-name=\"Page:Discourses of Epictetus.djvu/288\" data-page-index=\"288\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Discourses&#95;of&#95;Epictetus.djvu/288\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;288\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>not. If we were horses, would you say, My father was swifter? I have much barley and fodder, or elegant neck ornaments. If then while you were saying this, said, Be it so: let us run then. Well, is there nothing in a man such as running in a horse, by which it will be known which is superior and inferior? Is there not modesty (ald\u00e8s), fidelity, justice? Show yourself superior in these, that you may be superior as a man. If you tell me that you can kick violently, I also will say to you, that you are proud of that which is the act of an ass. \n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\"><h2 id=\"Footnotes\">Footnotes</h2><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments/Book_3/Chapter_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Footnotes\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14922110\">.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs{font-size:83%;clear:both;line-height:1.25;margin-top:0.3em}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs ol.references{margin-top:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs-ruled{border-top:1px solid;padding-top:0.3em}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs ol.references.uksi{margin-top:0;list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs ol.references.uksi li::marker{font-weight:bold}</style>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\"><div class=\"reflist wst-smallrefs\" style=\"\">\n<ol class=\"references\">\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-1\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-1\">\u2191</a></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">All the MSS. have 'good' (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r15431475\" /><span class=\"wst-lang wst-lang-grc\" lang=\"grc\" dir=\"ltr\">\u03ba\u03b1\u03bb\u03bf\u03af</span>), which the critics have properly corrected. As to <link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r15431475\" /><span class=\"wst-lang wst-lang-grc\" lang=\"grc\" dir=\"ltr\">\u03c3\u03ba\u03cc\u03c0\u03b5\u03b9</span> see Schweig.'s note.</span>\n</li>\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-2\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-2\">\u2191</a></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">This section is not easy to translate.</span>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nParsed by mw\u2010web.codfw.main\u201095db66ff4\u2010jtcbm\nCached time: 20260701155238\nCache expiry: 2592000\nCache expiry source: Module:Header (os.date(%Y))\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [vary\u2010page\u2010id, vary\u2010revision\u2010sha1]\nCPU time usage: 0.056 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.135 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 347/1000000\nRevision size: 496/2097152 bytes\nPost\u2010expand include size: 9623/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 836/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 11/100\nExpensive parser function count: 4/500\nUnstrip recursion depth: 1/20\nUnstrip post\u2010expand size: 8619/5000000 bytes\nLua time usage: 0.027/10.000 seconds\nLua memory usage: 1479641/52428800 bytes\nNumber of Wikibase entities loaded: 0/500\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%  129.755      1 -total\n 53.41%   69.302      1 Template:Header\n 10.57%   13.709      2 Temp